## Database design and test inventory

- HRB-01/02: fp_document_requests primary key (owner_id,key) reserves the normalized
  {title,status} body before document insertion. ON CONFLICT waits for the transaction
  winner; identical content returns its stored document, different content conflicts.
  No automatic HTTP retry. Verify sequential replay, same-key races and per-user keys.
- HRB-03/04: HTTP uses a fresh client with legacy anon key and the incoming Bearer JWT;
  getUser(token) checks the local Auth server before reading the body. DB derives owner
  from auth.uid(). fp_documents SELECT is granted only to authenticated, with own-row
  RLS. No table writes or audit/request reads for anon/authenticated. All tables have RLS.
  fp_create_document is SECURITY DEFINER, owned by the migration DB owner, search_path='';
  PUBLIC/anon EXECUTE revoked, authenticated EXECUTE granted. Definer elevation exists
  only to perform the audited transaction; no owner connection or service-role key in HTTP.
  Verify direct reads for both users and anonymous/write/RPC denial, plus catalog grants.
- HRB-05/06/09: one transaction reserves key, inserts document, inserts bounded audit
  {document_id,owner_id,request_key,event:'created',created_at}, stores result. Fault after
  document insertion raises P0001; DB rolls everything back, HTTP emits internal_error.
  Missing/other-owner GET is not_found. Failed writes never expose provider text. Verify
  failure counts, successful same-key recovery, and state with a fresh DB connection.
- HRB-08/11/12: auth precedes body processing, real bytes <=4096, JSON-only strict body,
  key allowlist, JSON errors and no-store. SQL additionally checks normalized body,
  title/status/key so direct RPC cannot bypass domain validation. Test malformed data,
  unsupported paths, authentication, byte limit (including chunked) and exact DTOs.
- HRB-07 is inapplicable: no money/bigint. HRB-10 is limited to create/read; no additional
  lifecycle or session-revocation guarantees were requested.

## Next consumer boundary

Use server-side fetch to http://127.0.0.1:18510 with Authorization: Bearer <user access
JWT>, cache:'no-store', and no cookies forwarded to this API. Never send the legacy anon
key as the user's access token. This fixture intentionally uses legacy key compatibility;
keep service-role keys and DB owner connection out of the consumer's user data path.
Create a request-scoped Supabase user client (no shared mutable session), configured
with persistSession:false and autoRefreshToken:false when forwarding an existing token.
Obtain tokens through Auth signInWithPassword; do not log password/session values.
Use getUser(token) for this fixture's Auth-server identity verification. getSession's
embedded user alone is not authorization. A future cookie-based Next app must separately
implement and verify its version-compatible SSR refresh/cookie boundary; that is not
implemented or proven here.

POST /documents: application/json; {title:string,status?:'draft'|'published'} only;
Idempotency-Key: 1..64 ASCII letters/digits/_/-. Generate once per logical create and
retain the same key and content after network loss or unknown outcome. Title trims and
status defaults to draft. 201 {document,replayed:false}; repeat 200 with same document
and replayed:true; changed content 409 idempotency_conflict. Never replace the key merely
because a response was lost. Concurrent equal requests create one document and one audit.
GET /documents/<UUID>: 200 {document} or 404 not_found for missing/other owner's row.
Document = {id:UUID,owner_id:UUID,title:string,status:'draft'|'published'}.
400 invalid_request, 401 unauthorized, 413 too_large, 415 unsupported_media_type,
500 internal_error; every error is {error:code}. All document responses are JSON/no-store.
Direct Data API own SELECT on fp_documents is supported; all creates must use the API
or the audited fp_create_document RPC. RPC requires normalized {title,status}, p_key,
and p_fail:false. X-Fixture-Fail:after_document / p_fail:true are local synthetic test
facilities only, not a deployment feature.

